diff --git a/doc/Manual/ManualCreditControl.html b/doc/Manual/ManualCreditControl.html
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..5168d00
--- /dev/null
+++ b/doc/Manual/ManualCreditControl.html
@@ -0,0 +1,66 @@
+<a id="CreditControl"><h1>Credit Control</h1></a>
+<p>
+Credit control applies the same rules to every customer, whichever way the order arrives: entered by the sales office, placed by the customer in the web shop, sent electronically (EDI) or created from a standing order. Everything credit control decides - reviews, stop and watch, limit changes, reminders, charges, waivers and write-offs - is written to the customer's credit control history, with who did it, when and why. History rows cannot be changed or deleted.
+</p>
+<p>
+Credit control uses two security tokens: <b>Credit Control</b> (token 21) for the credit controller and the deputy, and <b>Credit Control approvals</b> (token 22) for the Finance Director, who approves large write-offs and sets the credit control settings. Give the tokens to security roles in the Access Permissions Maintenance. Anyone without token 21 can see credit information but cannot change the credit status or the credit limit of a customer, in Customer Credit Control or in Customer maintenance. Sales reps (users set up for one sales person) only see their own customers.
+</p>
+
+<h2>Setting up</h2>
+<ol>
+<li>In Credit Status Maintenance define at least one credit status that is <i>On stop</i> and one that is <i>On watch</i>, besides the normal status (the upgrade adds them when they are missing).</li>
+<li>In Company Preferences choose the GL accounts for <i>interest income</i>, <i>late fee income</i> and <i>bad debts</i>.</li>
+<li>In Credit Control Settings (system reports menu) set the standard review period, the annual interest rate, the late fee, the days overdue before charges apply, the write-off approval limit, and edit the reminder letter stages: how many days overdue triggers each stage and the wording of each letter. Letters are in one language only.</li>
+<li>In System Parameters, <i>Check Credit Limits</i> must be set to Warn or Prohibit for the credit limit checks. When it is set to Warn, orders that take a customer over the limit are held for credit control (this can be switched off in Credit Control Settings).</li>
+<li>At go-live every customer has a next review date. Use the utility <i>Import Credit Status</i> (csv: customer code, credit status code, credit limit, next review date) to put customers from the old stop list on stop and to load limits and review dates.</li>
+</ol>
+
+<h2>Credit reviews</h2>
+<p>
+Select the customer in Customer Credit Control. Record the review: the outcome (limit confirmed, raised, reduced, put on watch, put on stop), the new limit, notes and the next review date. The new limit and status apply at once, and the review is kept in the history. New customers get a next review date automatically, one standard review period after the account is opened. The credit control report lists customers whose review is due.
+</p>
+
+<h2>Stop and watch</h2>
+<p>
+A customer <b>on stop</b> cannot be given an order by any route, and goods cannot be dispatched or invoiced on orders already taken, unless credit control releases them in Orders Held for Credit Control. Standing orders for a customer on stop are not created (they are tried again at the next run, once the stop is lifted), and electronic orders are refused. A customer in the web shop is told the account is on hold and to contact us.
+</p>
+<p>
+A customer <b>on watch</b> can be supplied, but anyone taking an order is warned, and the orders appear on the Orders Held for Credit Control list. Customers are never shown that they are on watch. The credit status is shown on the customer selection screen, the customer account and order entry.
+</p>
+<p>
+Orders that take a customer over their credit limit are saved but held, and cannot be dispatched until credit control releases them.
+</p>
+<p>
+To change the status of a customer use <i>Change credit status</i> with a reason. Taking a customer off stop needs a reason too. Tick invoices as <i>disputed</i> to leave them out of reminders and interest until the dispute is settled. Notes about debt collection or legal action handled outside the system can be added to the history.
+</p>
+
+<h2>Reminder letters</h2>
+<p>
+Run the Overdue Reminder Run (for example weekly). It lists each overdue customer with the stage of letter due: the highest stage whose days overdue has been reached. Tick <i>Leave out</i> for customers who must not be chased in this run, then email the reminders. A customer who has paid gets nothing, a customer is never sent the same stage twice within the minimum number of days, and disputed invoices are never chased. The letters go to the customer's contacts that are set to receive statements. Customers without an email address are listed so that the letter can be posted, and the run can then record the letters as posted. Each reminder is recorded with the date, stage, invoices and amount, and customers see them in their account.
+</p>
+
+<h2>Interest and late fees</h2>
+<p>
+At month end open Interest and Late Fees, choose the month and review the list of what will be charged to whom. Interest is one twelfth of the annual rate on invoices overdue by more than the charge days (interest is never charged on earlier interest and fees); the late fee is a fixed fee per customer per month, converted to the customer's currency. Customers exempt from interest or fees are marked in Customer Credit Control. Confirm to post the charges as separate items on the customer account (they show on the next statement) and to the general ledger. The same month cannot be charged twice for a customer. A charge can be waived with a reason; the waiver is posted to the ledger too.
+</p>
+
+<h2>Bad debt write-offs</h2>
+<p>
+Request a write-off of all or part of a customer's open invoices with a reason. Write-offs up to the approval limit are posted at once; larger ones wait until the Finance Director approves them on the same screen. A write-off reduces the customer's balance, is allocated to the oldest open invoices, and posts to the bad debt account; the customer is put on stop unless the option is cleared. The report at the bottom of the screen lists write-offs over a period with who requested and approved them.
+</p>
+
+<h2>Credit control report</h2>
+<p>
+Lists customers due or overdue for a credit review, customers over their credit limit, and customers on watch or on stop, filtered by sales person and currency, with totals in the home currency. The aged debtors report also shows each customer's credit status, credit limit and the last reminder stage sent.
+</p>
+
+<h2>Month end reconciliation</h2>
+<p>
+The Debtors Control Reconciliation compares the debtors control account in the general ledger with the customer transactions and balances, and lists the transactions and journals that explain a difference.
+</p>
+
+<h2>Notes for sales staff</h2>
+<p>
+<b>On watch</b>: you may take orders; you will see a warning. Talk to the customer before they order and contact credit control. <b>On stop</b>: you cannot take or release orders for the customer and you cannot lift the stop or raise the limit yourself. Orders held for credit control wait until credit control releases them. Contact the credit controller.
+</p>
+<!-- Help End: CreditControl -->
